The interim response is sufficient on the five items. Wednesday June 1 at 2=
:00 works for a call on Item 4; I will send the bridge details.<br />
<br />
So the call is productive, here is the staff's position stated plainly. Rul=
e 206(4)-2(d)(2)(ii) defines custody to include any arrangement under which=
 the adviser is authorized to withdraw client funds or securities maintaine=
d with a custodian upon its instruction to the custodian. That is exactly w=
hat Marchwood's advisory agreement authorizes at Section 6(c). The rule doe=
s not turn on whether the adviser could have taken more than its fee; it tu=
rns on whether the adviser can instruct the custodian to move client assets=
. It can.<br />
<br />
The exception the firm is relying on is paragraph (b)(3), and it does not r=
each this arrangement. That exception relieves an adviser of the paragraph =
(a)(4) surprise examination only where its custody arises solely as a conse=
quence of its authority to withdraw client funds to pay its advisory fee. S=
ection 6(c) is not drafted that way. It authorizes Marchwood to instruct th=
e custodian to withdraw or transfer funds or securities without limitation =
to the fee, and the staff reads the exception against the authority conferr=
ed rather than against the use the firm has so far made of it. The conseque=
nce is the surprise examination requirement in paragraph (a)(4), which Marc=
hwood has not met for 2010.<br />
